WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.367
-You brought me a gift?
-Yes, I did.

00:00:02.367 --> 00:00:03.734
-But you are the gift.

00:00:03.734 --> 00:00:06.033
[ Audience aws ]
-Well -- now you've got --

00:00:06.033 --> 00:00:07.901
-You want me to open it?
-Yes.

00:00:07.901 --> 00:00:09.834
Yes, I do.

00:00:09.834 --> 00:00:12.167
-I'm very excited about this.

00:00:12.167 --> 00:00:13.934
Ooh, what could it be?
It's heavy.

00:00:13.934 --> 00:00:15.834
Is this honey?

00:00:15.834 --> 00:00:17.167
-It's honey.

00:00:17.167 --> 00:00:20.734
-Honey from Debbie Harry, honey?

00:00:20.734 --> 00:00:23.701
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:23.701 --> 00:00:25.701
Thank you for this.
-You're welcome.

00:00:25.701 --> 00:00:27.667
-This is going to be
special honey for me.

00:00:27.667 --> 00:00:30.667
-It's from my own beehives.
[ Audience oohs ]

00:00:30.667 --> 00:00:31.901
That's even better, right?

00:00:31.901 --> 00:00:33.734
-Are you kidding me?
-No.

00:00:33.734 --> 00:00:36.567
-Oh, my gosh. Guys, check out
my eBay account right now.

00:00:36.567 --> 00:00:37.634
You won't even --
[ Laughter ]

00:00:37.634 --> 00:00:39.033
No, I'm not doing that.
-Okay, okay.

00:00:39.033 --> 00:00:41.334
-I would never. I would never.
I'm so excited about this.

00:00:41.334 --> 00:00:42.834
Do you remember the last time
we saw each other?

00:00:42.834 --> 00:00:45.000
-Yes.
-Because why?

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.400
The producers told you
backstage?

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:48.367
[ Laughter ]

00:00:48.367 --> 00:00:49.767
-No.
-We saw each other.

00:00:49.767 --> 00:00:52.200
You helped me out.
-Oh, no.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:54.300
-Yes, you really helped me
out of a pickle.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:58.000
I was at the "SNL" after-party
for the 40th anniversary

00:00:58.000 --> 00:00:59.300
of "Saturday Night Live."

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:01.267
And we were at the after-party
at the Plaza Hotel.

00:01:01.267 --> 00:01:04.033
And I was on stage
singing with the band.

00:01:04.033 --> 00:01:08.367
And I sang with Paul McCartney,
Taylor Swift,

00:01:08.367 --> 00:01:10.601
Miley Cyrus, Ariana Grande.

00:01:10.601 --> 00:01:14.033
And I go --
I saw you in the crowd.

00:01:14.033 --> 00:01:17.334
And I go, "Do you want to sing
'Tide Is High?'"

00:01:17.334 --> 00:01:19.400
And you're like,
"I can't believe this idiot

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.234
is asking me to sing."

00:01:21.234 --> 00:01:23.033
And I was like,
"Sorry to put you on the spot."

00:01:23.033 --> 00:01:24.634
But you came up
and you crushed it,

00:01:24.634 --> 00:01:27.267
"Tide Is High"
with Ariana Grande.

00:01:27.267 --> 00:01:30.067
-It's funny how people remember
things differently.

00:01:30.067 --> 00:01:32.901
[ Laughter ]

00:01:32.901 --> 00:01:35.234
-Wait.
Was that not what happened?

00:01:35.234 --> 00:01:37.300
-Not for me.
-Okay. Alright.

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:40.400
Well, I apologize for putting
you on the spot.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:43.834
But you crushed it that night.
It was wonderful.

00:01:43.834 --> 00:01:46.367
-It was totally memorable
and it was a great night.

00:01:46.367 --> 00:01:49.400
And an incredible party
and a great show.

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:52.100
-Ended with us jamming out
with Prince at the end.

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:53.634
-Yeah.
-The whole thing was crazy.

00:01:53.634 --> 00:01:55.400
It was just --
But thank you for doing that

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:56.868
'cause I was like --
I needed help

00:01:56.868 --> 00:01:58.601
and you saw the desperation
in my eyes.

00:01:58.601 --> 00:02:00.167
"Please get up and help me."

00:02:00.167 --> 00:02:02.767
You were like, "Alright, Jimmy,
let me show you how to do it."

00:02:02.767 --> 00:02:05.667
How was the tour?
You're working on a new album.

00:02:05.667 --> 00:02:07.067
Blondie has a new album
coming out.

00:02:07.067 --> 00:02:09.033
You had a tour this summer.

00:02:09.033 --> 00:02:10.968
How was it? Fun?

00:02:10.968 --> 00:02:14.634
-Well, we started, I think,
in the spring in the U.K.

00:02:14.634 --> 00:02:18.334
Then we moved over to, you know,
to this country.

00:02:18.334 --> 00:02:20.601
-Do you remember
when Blondie first came out

00:02:20.601 --> 00:02:22.300
and your first tour
and your first big tour,

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:24.334
and you were like, "Oh, this is
it. This is the big time."

00:02:24.334 --> 00:02:28.267
What's the difference
between then and now?

00:02:28.267 --> 00:02:29.701
-Bigger audiences.

00:02:29.701 --> 00:02:31.033
-Yeah, of course.

00:02:31.033 --> 00:02:32.934
Were you playing bars
back in the --

00:02:32.934 --> 00:02:35.834
-Everything.
We would play anything really.

00:02:35.834 --> 00:02:39.167
-Anything you could,
who would hire you.

00:02:39.167 --> 00:02:40.968
I love this box set, by the way.

00:02:40.968 --> 00:02:42.267
You did a great job with it.

00:02:42.267 --> 00:02:43.934
There's books in here.

00:02:43.934 --> 00:02:46.234
There's every vinyl.
There's 10-inch vinyls.

00:02:46.234 --> 00:02:48.734
There's every album.
-Yeah.

00:02:48.734 --> 00:02:51.000
-Do you have a favorite album?
That's a tough thing to ask.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:53.467
-Hmm. Maybe "Autoamerican."

00:02:53.467 --> 00:02:55.400
-Oh, yeah.
-I don't know.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:58.567
It's hard because there's so
many songs that we never play.

00:02:58.567 --> 00:03:00.200
-But how do you just
be this cool?

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:03.267
I mean, you were unbelievable.

00:03:03.267 --> 00:03:05.734
Were you always --
Did you always have the --

00:03:05.734 --> 00:03:07.200
"I know I'm going to be cool"?

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:08.501
[ Laughter ]

00:03:08.501 --> 00:03:10.234
Did you always have --
as a little kid?

00:03:10.234 --> 00:03:13.334
-No. No, no. I was very shy.

00:03:13.334 --> 00:03:15.200
-You were?
-Yes, I still am.

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:16.434
-Oh, no!

00:03:16.434 --> 00:03:19.133
[ Laughter ]
Oh, honey, don't be shy.

00:03:19.133 --> 00:03:20.934
[ Laughter ]

00:03:20.934 --> 00:03:22.801
I want to ask you because
obviously

00:03:22.801 --> 00:03:25.100
you became the face of punk.

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:27.167
You're the face of fashion.

00:03:27.167 --> 00:03:28.868
Always fashion forward.

00:03:28.868 --> 00:03:32.100
Here's you in a --
Here's why I like --

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:33.567
There's a funny story to this.

00:03:33.567 --> 00:03:34.734
Because you look gorgeous,
right,

00:03:34.734 --> 00:03:37.133
but what is the story
behind the dress?

00:03:37.133 --> 00:03:41.501
-The dress was a pillowcase.

00:03:41.501 --> 00:03:44.501
-This is how you end up
being Debbie Harry.

00:03:44.501 --> 00:03:46.367
-Well, I was much smaller.

00:03:46.367 --> 00:03:48.968
[ Laughter ]

00:03:48.968 --> 00:03:51.501
-How did you end up making
a pillowcase into a dress?

00:03:51.501 --> 00:03:53.834
-My landlord found it
in the street and he said,

00:03:53.834 --> 00:03:55.334
"Oh, this would be
a good dress."

00:03:55.334 --> 00:03:59.834
And he sort of, you know,
held it -- he held it up.

00:03:59.834 --> 00:04:02.767
And he said, "You know,
just cut here.

00:04:02.767 --> 00:04:05.701
And look, it's already there."
-[ Laughs ] Oh, my God.

00:04:05.701 --> 00:04:07.968
He found it in the street
and gave you a pillowcase.

00:04:07.968 --> 00:04:10.601
And you go, "Yeah, I'll make
this into an iconic dress."

00:04:10.601 --> 00:04:12.133
Wow.
-Yeah.

00:04:12.133 --> 00:04:15.033
-I wanted to ask you about
another photo that you did.

00:04:15.033 --> 00:04:17.067
You have ton of photo shoots
and you look gorgeous.

00:04:17.067 --> 00:04:20.501
But can you explain to me that
you were on the cover of

00:04:20.501 --> 00:04:21.968
"Wrestling Magazine."

00:04:21.968 --> 00:04:23.767
[ Laughter ]

00:04:23.767 --> 00:04:26.901
How did this happen?
Why did this happen?

00:04:26.901 --> 00:04:29.601
And what was going on here?

00:04:29.601 --> 00:04:34.968
-Well, we -- I met this woman
in my neighborhood while

00:04:34.968 --> 00:04:37.501
we were living on 21st Street.

00:04:37.501 --> 00:04:40.601
And we always said hello.
Her name was Nancy Moon.

00:04:40.601 --> 00:04:42.701
-Cool name.
-And we got to talking

00:04:42.701 --> 00:04:49.534
and she said that she was the
press person, I guess, for --

00:04:49.534 --> 00:04:53.868
I don't know if it was for the
Garden or for Vince McMahon.

00:04:53.868 --> 00:04:56.300
So, she said,
"Any time you want to go."

00:04:56.300 --> 00:04:59.868
And of course, Chris said,
"You know, let's go."

00:04:59.868 --> 00:05:01.567
So we went all the time.

00:05:01.567 --> 00:05:03.067
-You were giant wrestling fans?
-Yeah.

00:05:03.067 --> 00:05:04.434
-And show up to all the things.

00:05:04.434 --> 00:05:06.067
-Yeah, we went to hundreds of
them.

00:05:06.067 --> 00:05:08.167
-Just because you could get in?
-Yeah.

00:05:08.167 --> 00:05:10.133
Well, no, because we watched it
on TV.

00:05:10.133 --> 00:05:13.467
And it's much better live,
you know?

00:05:13.467 --> 00:05:15.834
-I just love that you're
a giant wrestling fan.

00:05:15.834 --> 00:05:17.634
It's so fun.
How was Andre the Giant?

00:05:17.634 --> 00:05:19.734
-Andre was very nice, very nice.

00:05:19.734 --> 00:05:21.734
Very tall, as you can see.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:21.734 --> 00:05:25.167
And he shook my hand
and my hand disappeared.

00:05:25.167 --> 00:05:27.033
[ Laughter ]

00:05:27.033 --> 00:05:31.801
I felt so short. I stood on
my toes and it didn't help.

00:05:31.801 --> 00:05:33.033
[ Laughter ]

00:05:33.033 --> 00:05:34.968
-It didn't help at all,
not even on your toes.

00:05:34.968 --> 00:05:38.567
-No.
-But this led you to working
with Andy Kaufman.

00:05:38.567 --> 00:05:40.400
-Yeah.
-How?

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:42.067
Because he was doing
his whole wrestling thing?

00:05:42.067 --> 00:05:45.767
-Well, you know, I guess
coincidentally, I don't know.

00:05:45.767 --> 00:05:48.534
A play came over from the U.K.

00:05:48.534 --> 00:05:51.167
It was called "Trafford Tanzi."

00:05:51.167 --> 00:05:54.167
And Trafford I think is
outside of Manchester.

00:05:54.167 --> 00:05:56.667
-So, it was a Broadway show?
-Yeah.

00:05:56.667 --> 00:05:58.434
It opened and closed
in one night, so...

00:05:58.434 --> 00:06:00.601
-Congratulations.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:00.601 --> 00:06:03.067
That's a record.
-One of those, one of those.

00:06:03.067 --> 00:06:05.968
-I've never seen this photo
of you and Andy Kaufman.

00:06:05.968 --> 00:06:07.334
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:07.334 --> 00:06:09.734
Oh, he was a sweetheart.
-He was?

00:06:09.734 --> 00:06:11.701
-Yeah.
-Yeah, he was really funny.

00:06:11.701 --> 00:06:13.467
Unbelievable.

00:06:13.467 --> 00:06:15.501
Reading through the books
and looking through all this,

00:06:15.501 --> 00:06:17.434
there's so many fun, cool things
in here.

00:06:17.434 --> 00:06:19.734
Little tidbits, but one thing,
"Heart of Glass,"

00:06:19.734 --> 00:06:21.367
one of my favorite songs,

00:06:21.367 --> 00:06:23.667
originally, it wasn't titled
"Heart of Glass."

00:06:23.667 --> 00:06:27.300
-No, it was "Once I Had a Love."

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:29.767
And it was "soon turned out,
it was a pain in the ass."

00:06:29.767 --> 00:06:32.100
I don't know if it was
"the pain in the ass" song.

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:33.901
-It was going to be called
"Pain in the Ass"?

00:06:33.901 --> 00:06:38.133
-And I guess it was
when we started

00:06:38.133 --> 00:06:40.601
having a record contract,
they said --

00:06:40.601 --> 00:06:41.801
-"Please don't do that."

00:06:41.801 --> 00:06:43.000
[ Laughter ]

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:44.067
"Please change that lyric.
Please."

00:06:44.067 --> 00:06:45.634
-Can't have that.
-Yeah.

00:06:45.634 --> 00:06:47.601
And so then who came up with
"Heart of Glass?"

00:06:47.601 --> 00:06:49.467
-I think it was Chris.

00:06:49.467 --> 00:06:52.667
And I think it was just,
you know, his wisdom

00:06:52.667 --> 00:06:54.234
or his, you know, brilliance.

00:06:54.234 --> 00:06:57.667
And he just said,
"Well, it's 'heart of glass.'

00:06:57.667 --> 00:06:59.801
We'll say heart of glass."
And then we found out later

00:06:59.801 --> 00:07:02.434
that there was a film named
"Heart of Glass."

00:07:02.434 --> 00:07:05.367
-It's interesting talking to you
because now I've known you

00:07:05.367 --> 00:07:06.567
and I've run into you
at different things.

00:07:06.567 --> 00:07:10.434
But you come off so cool like,
"Yeah."

00:07:10.434 --> 00:07:12.434
But you're Blondie.
You're Debbie Harry.

00:07:12.434 --> 00:07:14.267
You're an icon.
-It's all a mistake.

00:07:14.267 --> 00:07:16.868
[ Laughter ]
-It's all a mistake?

00:07:16.868 --> 00:07:18.734
And who made the mistake? Us?

00:07:18.734 --> 00:07:20.300
[ Laughter ]

00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:22.234
Yeah, we trumped it right now.

00:07:22.234 --> 00:07:24.801
I love you.
I'm honored to have you here.

00:07:24.801 --> 00:07:27.167
Please come back
even if you have nothing.

00:07:27.167 --> 00:07:29.167
Just come with more honey.
That's all I ask.

00:07:29.167 --> 00:07:30.868
Debbie Harry, everybody.

00:07:30.868 --> 00:07:32.767
Blondie, the new box set
right here.

00:07:32.767 --> 00:07:34.467
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:34.467 --> 00:07:40.901
"Against the Odds 1974-1982"
available now.

00:07:40.901 --> 00:07:42.467
Debbie Harry!

00:07:42.467 --> 00:07:44.467
We'll be right back with
Lea Michele. Stick around.

